        "description": "The development of a Solar Powered Vertical Take-Off and Landing (VTOL) drone represents a significant step toward achieving sustainable and energy-efficient unmanned aerial systems. Unlike conventional drones that rely solely on battery power, the proposed design integrates flexible solar panels onto the wings to harness renewable solar energy for in-flight battery charging and endurance enhancement.\r\n\r\nA VTOL configuration was selected to combine the advantages of rotary-wing hovering and fixed-wing forward flight, ensuring both operational flexibility and extended range. Initial tests demonstrated that flexible solar panels could successfully recharge the onboard battery, compensating for energy consumption during flight. Due to the larger size of the panels, scaling the VTOL platform is suggested to achieve optimal integration and improved energy capture.\r\n\r\nThis approach not only reduces dependency on ground-based charging infrastructure but also opens pathways for long-duration missions in applications such as environmental monitoring, surveillance, disaster management, and precision agriculture. The concept highlights the potential of merging renewable energy technology with UAV systems, contributing to the advancement of green aviation solutions.",
